  "enhanced_information": "Erythropoietin-stimulating agents (ESAs), such as darbepoetin, are indicated for the treatment of anemia in patients with chronic kidney disease (CKD) due to decreased erythropoietin production. ESAs can help increase hemoglobin levels, but they should be used cautiously, particularly when hemoglobin levels are < 10 g/dL, as higher levels may increase the risk of adverse events. It is also essential to ensure adequate iron levels for the efficacy of ESAs, as low iron can limit their effectiveness. The use of ESAs is not primarily aimed at improving survival in cancer patients, and the highest-tolerated dose is not recommended in CKD management. Therefore, the most accurate statement regarding the use of ESAs in this context is that they should only be used when hemoglobin is < 10 g/dL."
